Goal:
The setuid binary in the home directory can be used to read the password for the next level. 

Connect:
ssh bandit19@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

Approach:
. The setuid file that is provided to us also has executable permissions which means we can execute
  that file.
. Once I executed that file without any parameters it prompted the syntax for the file.
. From this prompt i understood this is an executable file which performs commands as the 
  user bandit20.
. After this knowledge it was easy to get the password for the next level as the location of the 
  file containing the password was informed to us.

Command:
./bandit20-do cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit20

Breakdown:
./bandit20-do -> The realtive path indicates that execute the file in the current dir.
cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit20 -> This is the command that we want the executable file to perform
                                  which is to extract the content of that file which contains the 
                                  password for the next level.

Result:
We are granted the password upon this command's execution.

Key Takeaway:
This room introduced me to setuid files, which run with the permissions of the file's 
owner rather than the user executing it. In this case, the owner is bandit20, so the binary 
runs any command as bandit20 regardless of who invokes it.
